1
00:00:02,080 --> 00:00:07,420
Hi, I'm Belle. Let me show you how AI can
help with a literature review—and how we

2
00:00:07,420 --> 00:00:08,580
check its work.

3
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:12,260
You've screened the titles and abstracts.

4
00:00:12,260 --> 00:00:13,740
Now you need the full texts.

5
00:00:14,380 --> 00:00:19,080
The titles and DOIs are already in your
database export.

6
00:00:19,540 --> 00:00:23,400
But you're still finding and downloading
each paper, one by one.

7
00:00:24,080 --> 00:00:28,480
Then, once your team has developed a
codebook, you're finding the same kinds of

8
00:00:28,480 --> 00:00:32,700
information and filling in the same coding
table, paper after paper.

9
00:00:33,560 --> 00:00:34,880
And there's the teamwork.

10
00:00:35,220 --> 00:00:40,840
We need to divide up the papers or fields,
send out review files,

11
00:00:40,880 --> 00:00:42,720
and collect everyone's feedback.

12
00:00:43,340 --> 00:00:48,080
We need to know who's finished and which
answers we still disagree on.

13
00:00:49,660 --> 00:00:51,980
AI could help with all of this.

14
00:00:52,740 --> 00:00:56,200
But how do I know it's getting things
right?

15
00:00:57,360 --> 00:00:59,060
Did it find the right paper?

16
00:00:59,900 --> 00:01:02,460
Did it understand the text and follow our
codebook?

17
00:01:03,160 --> 00:01:06,880
And how do we check its answers without
starting from scratch?

18
00:01:08,560 --> 00:01:12,640
That's why I wanted a workflow where AI
prepares the work,

19
00:01:12,880 --> 00:01:17,360
and we can see the evidence, check the
answers, and make corrections.

20
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:21,220
I built two skills around that.

21
00:01:21,680 --> 00:01:22,940
Let me show you.

22
00:01:23,380 --> 00:01:26,900
Here's one item AI has prepared for me to
check.

23
00:01:27,660 --> 00:01:33,040
I click the page number, and the PDF jumps
to

24
00:01:33,040 --> 00:01:38,500
that page. If it finds the matching quote,
it highlights

25
00:01:38,500 --> 00:01:39,380
it too.

26
00:01:40,040 --> 00:01:45,000
I read it, check the context, and decide:
does this look right,

27
00:01:45,380 --> 00:01:47,580
or does something need to change?

28
00:01:48,440 --> 00:01:52,480
A skill is a set of instructions an AI
agent can follow.

29
00:01:53,660 --> 00:01:57,180
One helps me find and check PDFs.

30
00:01:59,880 --> 00:02:03,440
The other uses our rules to prepare the
review work,

31
00:02:04,120 --> 00:02:07,120
then helps us collect and compare feedback.

32
00:02:08,360 --> 00:02:10,700
Let's start with the PDFs.

33
00:02:11,620 --> 00:02:14,980
I give the AI the list from our initial
screening,

34
00:02:15,100 --> 00:02:20,400
with the titles, DOIs where available, and
any PDFs I already have.

35
00:02:20,640 --> 00:02:25,960
Then I ask it to find the missing full
texts and check that each file matches the

36
00:02:25,960 --> 00:02:27,120
paper on the list.

37
00:02:28,020 --> 00:02:33,720
It tries different places: open-access
copies, publisher websites,

38
00:02:33,960 --> 00:02:36,720
or resources through my university library.

39
00:02:37,180 --> 00:02:42,400
It keeps track of what it tried, so we can
pick up where we left off.

40
00:02:43,520 --> 00:02:47,620
In my own work, it found library access I
didn't know I had.

41
00:02:48,580 --> 00:02:50,320
Sometimes I still need to step in.

42
00:02:50,440 --> 00:02:53,460
Maybe I need to log in, or download a file
myself.

43
00:02:53,700 --> 00:02:59,220
It tells me which paper, where to go, and
where to put the file afterward.

44
00:03:00,100 --> 00:03:03,520
Once I give it the file, it checks it.

45
00:03:03,520 --> 00:03:05,400
Is this the right paper?

46
00:03:05,960 --> 00:03:08,960
The right version? Is anything missing?

47
00:03:09,740 --> 00:03:14,340
If it's still unsure, that stays on the
list for us to check.

48
00:03:15,620 --> 00:03:17,040
Now we have the papers.

49
00:03:17,300 --> 00:03:21,460
Next comes full-text screening and, for the
papers we keep,

50
00:03:22,020 --> 00:03:23,680
coding the information we need.

51
00:03:25,420 --> 00:03:27,780
First, we need our own rules.

52
00:03:27,900 --> 00:03:29,400
What are we looking for?

53
00:03:29,540 --> 00:03:32,680
What counts? What do we need to record?

54
00:03:34,660 --> 00:03:37,680
That's our codebook. We develop it
ourselves.

55
00:03:39,120 --> 00:03:43,440
For example, we might start with ten papers
and work through them together.

56
00:03:43,780 --> 00:03:46,200
If a rule is unclear, we change it.

57
00:03:46,420 --> 00:03:51,560
Once we're comfortable with the rules, AI
can find the relevant passages

58
00:03:52,080 --> 00:03:54,520
and draft the coding for the next batch.

59
00:03:54,840 --> 00:03:58,820
We check the answers against the rules and
the original text.

60
00:04:00,380 --> 00:04:01,960
Ten is just an example.

61
00:04:03,580 --> 00:04:07,400
You choose the batch size and how much help
you want from AI.

62
00:04:09,720 --> 00:04:11,740
We can also choose how people review.

63
00:04:12,360 --> 00:04:15,860
Sometimes I want a colleague to check the
AI's suggestions.

64
00:04:16,040 --> 00:04:19,340
Other times, I want them to make their own
judgment first.

65
00:04:19,760 --> 00:04:24,020
In that case, I leave out the AI's
suggestions and everyone else's feedback.

66
00:04:25,660 --> 00:04:28,460
I can give each person a specific task.

67
00:04:29,040 --> 00:04:33,860
For example, one colleague checks methods,
while another checks concepts.

68
00:04:34,040 --> 00:04:36,420
Or we can assign different sets of papers.

69
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:41,600
Each person gets a review package with
their papers, fields,

70
00:04:41,600 --> 00:04:46,840
and instructions. They open it in their
browser and send the feedback file back

71
00:04:46,840 --> 00:04:50,340
when they're done. They don't need their
own AI setup.

72
00:04:51,400 --> 00:04:55,340
I can then ask AI to check what's been
returned,

73
00:04:55,520 --> 00:05:00,600
flag missing responses, and bring the
different answers together.

74
00:05:01,140 --> 00:05:05,340
Our team goes back to the evidence to work
through any disagreements.

75
00:05:06,220 --> 00:05:08,380
So people are involved throughout.

76
00:05:08,720 --> 00:05:12,360
We develop the rules, read the evidence,
and work through disagreements.

77
00:05:13,040 --> 00:05:16,900
AI helps prepare and organize the work
around that.

78
00:05:17,900 --> 00:05:19,860
Now let's look at the actual page.

79
00:05:20,120 --> 00:05:22,040
There are three main areas.

80
00:05:22,200 --> 00:05:23,700
Papers on the left.

81
00:05:24,120 --> 00:05:26,800
The items we're checking in the middle.

82
00:05:26,800 --> 00:05:28,300
And the PDF on the right.

83
00:05:28,820 --> 00:05:31,120
At the top left, I can filter the list.

84
00:05:31,120 --> 00:05:35,600
I can see everything, pick up the papers
that still need checking,

85
00:05:35,900 --> 00:05:38,840
or look at the included and excluded
papers.

86
00:05:40,260 --> 00:05:42,900
Let's open an excluded paper first.

87
00:05:43,140 --> 00:05:45,040
We still need to check the reason.

88
00:05:45,280 --> 00:05:46,760
Why was it excluded?

89
00:05:47,060 --> 00:05:49,000
Does the paper support that decision?

90
00:05:50,300 --> 00:05:53,600
Now let's open an included one.

91
00:05:54,140 --> 00:05:58,420
Under Screening, I can expand these
sections and check each requirement.

92
00:05:58,960 --> 00:06:01,960
I can see the reason and where it came
from.

93
00:06:02,540 --> 00:06:05,280
These requirements come from our own
codebook.

94
00:06:06,240 --> 00:06:09,640
In this example, we're looking at review
papers.

95
00:06:09,980 --> 00:06:13,300
Review profile gives us the main question
and methods.

96
00:06:13,740 --> 00:06:19,140
Concepts shows how the paper talks about
the ideas we're interested in.

97
00:06:19,140 --> 00:06:23,660
And Review-level synthesis brings together
what this particular review says.

98
00:06:24,760 --> 00:06:29,880
Later, we still need to compare across
reviews to build our own conclusions.

99
00:06:30,980 --> 00:06:33,360
Let's check one item properly.

100
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:37,680
This one points to PDF page eighteen.

101
00:06:37,680 --> 00:06:39,420
I click it, and here it is.

102
00:06:39,420 --> 00:06:41,760
The highlight helps me find the sentence.

103
00:06:41,860 --> 00:06:46,120
I still read around it to see whether we've
understood it correctly.

104
00:06:46,940 --> 00:06:50,900
If I need more room, I drag this divider
over.

105
00:06:50,900 --> 00:06:54,260
I can zoom in, scroll, or turn the page.

106
00:06:54,280 --> 00:06:56,440
The item I'm checking stays in view.

107
00:06:56,980 --> 00:06:59,400
If it looks right, I click Correct.

108
00:06:59,760 --> 00:07:03,140
If I think it needs changing, I click
Revise.

109
00:07:03,780 --> 00:07:09,260
This box opens, and I explain what I would
change and why.

110
00:07:10,280 --> 00:07:14,760
When everything has feedback, it moves to
Verification complete.

111
00:07:15,640 --> 00:07:17,860
That means the feedback is in.

112
00:07:17,900 --> 00:07:20,480
We may still have disagreements to work
through.

113
00:07:20,680 --> 00:07:24,800
When I'm done, I export the feedback and
send the file back.

114
00:07:24,960 --> 00:07:28,040
AI can help organize the different
responses.

115
00:07:28,260 --> 00:07:31,480
Then we go back to the evidence and decide
together.

116
00:07:32,960 --> 00:07:36,440
I'm using two of my own review projects as
examples.

117
00:07:37,820 --> 00:07:41,820
TALL looks at how technology supports
language learning.

118
00:07:41,880 --> 00:07:45,000
For that project, we're reviewing original
studies.

119
00:07:45,460 --> 00:07:50,520
The Agency project looks at existing
reviews of AI in education,

120
00:07:50,520 --> 00:07:53,980
focusing on learners' choices and control
over their learning.

121
00:07:54,460 --> 00:07:59,980
The questions and coding rules are
different, but the steps—finding papers,

122
00:08:00,080 --> 00:08:03,340
checking evidence, and collecting
feedback—can be reused.

123
00:08:04,640 --> 00:08:08,860
If you'd like to try it, start with what
you have: your paper list,

124
00:08:08,860 --> 00:08:11,400
your PDFs, and your research question.

125
00:08:11,820 --> 00:08:14,460
Bring your codebook too, if you already
have one.

126
00:08:14,940 --> 00:08:16,860
Still missing papers? Start there.

127
00:08:17,060 --> 00:08:18,640
Already have the PDFs?

128
00:08:18,640 --> 00:08:20,860
Start with the part you want help
reviewing.

129
00:08:21,420 --> 00:08:25,500
That's what I want this to help with: less
time going back and forth,

130
00:08:25,620 --> 00:08:29,540
and more time reading, thinking, and
talking through the work together.
